<section xml:id="sec-ch03-02-PartialDerivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Partial Derivatives of Scalar-Valued Functions</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Defining the derivative of a vector-valued function was simple because
      we only had one input variable (as in Calculus I).
    </p>

    <p>
      If we want to define a similar concept for functions of the form
      <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, we can do it by remembering that derivatives
      represent rates of change.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given a function <m>f(x)</m>, we know that
      <md>
        f^{\prime}(a)=\lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}
      </md>
      represents:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f</m> at the
            point <m>(a, f(a))</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The rate of change of <m>f</m> at <m>x=a</m> in the direction of
            the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-02-tangent-line">
      <caption>The curve y equals f of x, and its tangent line at x equals a.</caption>

      <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-01. -->
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-02-tangent-line" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-01.png">
            <shortdescription>The curve y equals f of x, and its tangent line at x equals a.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The curve <m>y=f(x)</m>, and its tangent line at <m>x=a</m>.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    <!--\caption{Graph of $f(x)$ (red) and its tangent line at $(a, f(a))$ (blue)}-->

    <p>
      Above we see the graph of <m>f(x)</m> (red) and its tangent line at
      <m>(a, f(a))</m> (blue).
    </p>

    <p>
      We build up on this definition and define the rate of change of
      <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, at the input ( <m>a, b</m> ), in the direction of
      the positive <m>x</m>-axis, as
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a, b)=\lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(a+h, b)-f(a, b)}{h}
      </md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that the change is happening in the <m>\mathbf{x}</m>-coordinate,
      and we leave the <m>\mathbf{y}</m> coordinate unchanged.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the rate of change of <m>f=x^{3}+y^{2}</m>, at the input
        <m>(1,1)</m>, in the direction of the positive <m>y</m>-axis.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The rate of change of <m>f</m> at the input <m>(1,1)</m> in the
        direction of the positive <m>y</m>-axis is found just like the rate
        of change in the direction of the positive <m>x</m>-axis, except now
        the change happens in the <m>y</m>-coordinate and we leave the
        <m>x</m>-coordinate unchanged. Using the corresponding limit:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(1,1) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(1, 1+h)-f(1,1)}{h} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{\left[ 1^3 + (1+h)^2 \right] - \left[ 1^3 + 1^2 \right]}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{1 + 1 + 2h + h^2 - 2}{h} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{2h + h^2}{h} = \lim_{h \to 0} \left( 2 + h \right) = 2</mrow>
        </md>
        So at the input <m>(1,1)</m>, the function <m>f</m> increases at a
        rate of 2 units per unit of change in <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Since in our definition of <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a, b)</m>,
      the <m>y</m>-coordinate is kept constant at <m>y=b</m>, we can
      interpret this geometrically as intersecting the surface
      <m>z=f(x, y)</m> with the plane <m>y=b</m> to get the level curve
      <m>f(x, b)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we have the surface <m>f=\sin (x+y)\cos (y-x)</m> and that
        we want to compute <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(0,0)</m>. Below
        is a picture of this surface intersected with the plane <m>y=0</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-02-ex-8-2-surface">
        <caption>A cross-section of z equals f of x and y, parallel to the xz-plane at y equals b, showing the partial derivative with respect to x at x equals a.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-03. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-02-ex-8-2-surface" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-03.png">
              <shortdescription>A cross-section of z equals f of x and y, parallel to the xz-plane at y equals b, showing the partial derivative with respect to x at x equals a.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A cross-section of <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, parallel to the <m>xz</m>-plane at <m>y=b</m>, showing the partial derivative with respect to <m>x</m> at <m>x=a</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        When we compute <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(0,0)</m>,
        geometrically, we are computing the slope of the tangent line to the
        curve <m>f(x, 0)</m> at the input <m>(0,0)</m>, as shown below:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-02-ex-8-2-tangent">
        <caption>The trace y equals b, and the tangent line of the trace at x equals a.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-03(1). -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-02-ex-8-2-tangent" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-03-1.png">
              <shortdescription>The trace y equals b, and the tangent line of the trace at x equals a.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The trace <m>y=b</m>, and the tangent line of the trace at <m>x=a</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      <!--\caption{The curve $f(x, 0)$ and the tangent line at $(0,0, f(0,0))$}-->

      <p>
        Above we see the curve <m>f(x, 0)</m> and the tangent line at
        <m>(0,0, f(0,0))</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    We summarize all of this information below.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Partial Derivative with Respect to <m>x</m></title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a scalar-valued function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, its partial
        derivative with respect to <m>x</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> (or
        at the point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m> is defined as
        <md>
          \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a, b)=f_{x}(a, b)=\lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(a+h, b)-f(a, b)}{h}
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We interpret <m>f_{x}(a, b)</m> as:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The slope of the tangent line to the curve <m>f(x, b)</m> at
              the point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The rate of change of <m>f</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> in
              the direction of the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      We have a similar definition for <m>f_{y}(a, b)</m> with a similar
      geometric interpretation.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Partial Derivative with Respect to <m>y</m></title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a scalar-valued function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, its partial
        derivative with respect to <m>y</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> (or
        at the point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m> is defined as
        <md>
          \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(a, b)=f_{y}(a, b)=\lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(a, b+h)-f(a, b)}{h}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        We interpret <m>f_{y}(a, b)</m> as:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The slope of the tangent line to the curve <m>f(a, y)</m> at
              the point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The rate of change of <m>f</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> in
              the direction of the positive <m>y</m>-axis.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

