  <p>
    The graphs above show only the plots; you can drag and zoom them. To see the expressions behind them, or to save a copy and create your own version of the graphs, open them in a new page from here:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <p>
      It is important we use proper notation when working with partial
      derivatives. Make sure to use the <q>partial</q> symbol and always
      refer to it as a partial derivative (not just a derivative).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Computing Partial Derivatives</title>

    <p>
      As is expected, we don't need to use limits every time we want to
      compute partial derivatives.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Partial Derivatives (Computational)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a scalar-valued function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, to compute
        <m>f_{x}</m>, take (algorithmically) the <q>usual</q> derivative of
        <m>f(x, y)</m> with respect to <m>x</m> whilst treating the variable
        <m>y</m> as a constant. (Similarly for <m>f_{y}</m> ).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-3">
    <statement>
      <p>Given <m>f(x, y)=(x+y)^2-e^y</m>, compute</p>

      <p>(a) <m>f_{x}</m>.</p>

      <p>(b) <m>f_{y}</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Treating <m>y</m> as a constant, the term <m>e^y</m> contains no
        <m>x</m> at all, so from the point of view of <m>x</m> it is just a
        fixed number (for example, if <m>y</m> were held at <m>y=1</m>, then
        <m>e^y = e^1 = e \approx 2.718</m>, a constant). The derivative of
        any constant is 0, so
        <md>
          \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( e^y \right) = 0
        </md>.
        The term <m>(x+y)^2</m> does depend on <m>x</m> and is
        differentiated using the (single-variable) chain rule, treating
        <m>y</m> as a constant. Putting the two pieces together:
        <md>
          f_x(x,y) = 2(x+y) \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x+y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( e^y \right) = 2(x+y)\cdot 1 - 0 = 2(x+y)
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Treating <m>x</m> as a constant, both terms now depend on <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          f_y(x,y) = 2(x+y) \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+y) - e^y = 2(x+y) - e^y
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-4">
    <statement>
      <p>Given <m>f(x, y)=\ln (x^4y^2+1)</m>, compute</p>

      <p>(a) <m>f_{x}</m>.</p>

      <p>(b) <m>f_{y}</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Treating <m>y</m> as a constant and using the chain rule with outer
        function <m>\ln</m>:
        <md>
          f_x(x,y) = \frac{1}{x^4y^2+1} \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( x^4y^2+1 \right) = \frac{4x^3y^2}{x^4y^2+1}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Treating <m>x</m> as a constant:
        <md>
          f_y(x,y) = \frac{1}{x^4y^2+1} \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left( x^4y^2+1 \right) = \frac{2x^4y}{x^4y^2+1}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the Cobb-Douglas Production function (L=labor, K = capital):
        <md>
          P(L,K)= 10L^{0.9}K^{0.1}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        1)Find the marginal productivity of labor and marginal productivity
        of capital when 20 units of labor and 10 units of capital are
        invested.
      </p>

      <p>
        2) For  this value of <m>L</m> is this Cobb-Douglas Production
        function an increasing or decreasing function of <m>K</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        3) For this value of <m>k</m> is this Cobb-Douglas Production
        function an increasing or decreasing function of <m>L</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The marginal productivity of labor is the partial derivative
        <m>P_L</m> and the marginal productivity of capital is the partial
        derivative <m>P_K</m>. Treating <m>K</m> and <m>L</m> as constants
        respectively:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>P_L(L,K) = 10(0.9)L^{-0.1}K^{0.1} = 9L^{-0.1}K^{0.1}</mrow>
          <mrow>P_K(L,K) = 10(0.1)L^{0.9}K^{-0.9} = L^{0.9}K^{-0.9}</mrow>
        </md>
        Evaluating at the input <m>(L,K) = (20,10)</m>:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>P_L(20,10) = 9(20)^{-0.1}(10)^{0.1} = 9\left( \frac{10}{20} \right)^{0.1} = \frac{9}{2^{0.1}} \approx 8.40</mrow>
          <mrow>P_K(20,10) = (20)^{0.9}(10)^{-0.9} = \left( \frac{20}{10} \right)^{0.9} = 2^{0.9} \approx 1.87</mrow>
        </md>
        So at this investment level, one additional unit of labor increases
        production by approximately 8.40 units, while one additional unit of
        capital increases production by approximately 1.87 units.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The rate of change of <m>P</m> with respect to <m>K</m> is
        <m>P_K = L^{0.9}K^{-0.9}</m>, which for <m>L=20</m> is
        <m>P_K = (20)^{0.9}K^{-0.9} \gt 0</m> for every <m>K \gt 0</m> (in
        particular <m>P_K(20,10) = 2^{0.9} \approx 1.87 \gt 0</m>). Since the
        partial derivative with respect to <m>K</m> is positive, the
        production function is an increasing function of <m>K</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The rate of change of <m>P</m> with respect to <m>L</m> is
        <m>P_L = 9L^{-0.1}K^{0.1}</m>, which for <m>K=10</m> is
        <m>P_L = 9(10)^{0.1}L^{-0.1} \gt 0</m> for every <m>L \gt 0</m> (in
        particular <m>P_L(20,10) \approx 8.40 \gt 0</m>). Since the partial
        derivative with respect to <m>L</m> is positive, the production
        function is an increasing function of <m>L</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>f(x, y)= x^{3} \sqrt y</m>, what would be the approximate
        effect on <m>f</m> of increasing <m>x</m> from 2 to 3 while keeping
        <m>y</m> fixed at 25 units? What about decreasing <m>y</m> from 25
        to 24 units while keeping <m>x</m> fixed at 2 units?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        For the first change we keep <m>y</m> fixed at 25 and increase
        <m>x</m> by <m>\Delta x = 1</m>, so we use the approximation
        <m>f(a+\Delta x, b) - f(a,b) \approx f_x(a,b) \Delta x</m>. Treating
        <m>y</m> as a constant, <m>f_x = 3x^2\sqrt{y}</m>, so
        <md>
          f_x(2,25) = 3(2)^2\sqrt{25} = 3(4)(5) = 60 \Rightarrow f(3,25) - f(2,25) \approx 60(1) = 60
        </md>
        and the approximate effect is an increase of about 60 units in
        <m>f</m>. For the second change we keep <m>x</m> fixed at 2 and
        decrease <m>y</m> by <m>\Delta y = -1</m>, so we use
        <m>f(a, b+\Delta y) - f(a,b) \approx f_y(a,b) \Delta y</m>. Treating
        <m>x</m> as a constant, <m>f_y = \frac{x^3}{2\sqrt{y}}</m>, so
        <md>
          f_y(2,25) = \frac{2^3}{2\sqrt{25}} = \frac{8}{10} = 0.8 \Rightarrow f(2,24) - f(2,25) \approx 0.8(-1) = -0.8
        </md>
        and the approximate effect is a decrease of about 0.8 units in
        <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Differentiability</title>

    <p>
      We can talk about a function <m>f(x, y)</m> being differentiable at an
      input <m>(a, b)</m>. However, it isn't as easy as just saying that
      <m>f_{x}(a, b)</m> and <m>f_{y}(a, b)</m> exist.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Differentiability</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A function <m>f(x, y)</m> is differentiable at the input
        <m>(a, b)</m> provided that <m>f_{x}(a, b)</m> and <m>f_{y}(a, b)</m>
        exist and that
        <md>
          f(x, y)=f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b)+E(x, y)
        </md>
        where <m>E(x, y)</m> approaches 0 as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m>
        and <m>y</m> approaches <m>b</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Geometrically, this means that <m>f(x, y)</m> is differentiable at
      the input <m>(a, b)</m> if as you zoom into the point
      ( <m>a, b, f(a, b)</m> ), the graph of <m>f</m> looks like a plane (is
      smooth).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/g2wwd8hvrj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">surface</url>
        <md>
          f(x, y)= \begin{cases}\frac{xy}{x^2+y^2} \amp (x,y)\not = (0,0) \\ 0, \amp (x,y) = (0,0) \end{cases}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        You can verify that <m>f_{x}(0,0)=f_{y}(0,0)=0</m> (they exist), but
        <m>f(x, y)</m> is not differentiable at <m>(0,0)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first verify that both partial derivatives exist at <m>(0,0)</m>
        using the limit definitions. Along the <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=0</m>)
        the function is <m>f(h,0) = \frac{h \cdot 0}{h^2+0^2} = 0</m> for
        every <m>h \neq 0</m>, so
        <md>
          f_x(0,0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(h,0)-f(0,0)}{h} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{0-0}{h} = 0
        </md>
        and similarly along the <m>y</m>-axis (<m>x=0</m>) we have
        <m>f(0,h) = 0</m>, so <m>f_y(0,0) = 0</m>. Both partial derivatives
        exist.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose <m>f</m> were differentiable at <m>(0,0)</m>. Then the
        error term
        <md>
          E(x,y) = f(x,y) - f(0,0) - f_x(0,0)(x-0) - f_y(0,0)(y-0) = f(x,y)
        </md>
        would have to approach 0 as <m>x</m> approaches 0 and <m>y</m>
        approaches 0. But if we approach the origin along the line
        <m>y=x</m>, then for every <m>x \neq 0</m>
        <md>
          E(x,x) = f(x,x) = \frac{x \cdot x}{x^2+x^2} = \frac{x^2}{2x^2} = \frac{1}{2}
        </md>
        which does not approach 0. Hence <m>f</m> is not differentiable at
        <m>(0,0)</m>. This matches the picture below: no matter how far we
        zoom in at the origin, the graph never flattens out to look like a
        plane.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-02-ex-8-7">
    <caption>A surface z equals f of x and y, from example 8.7</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_93272473a72f5ce70abdg-10. -->
