        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      As with traditional derivatives, you can compute partial derivatives
      multiple times (even with respect to different variables).
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Second-Order Partial Derivatives</title>

    <p>
      Below is a list of the four second-order partial derivatives of a
      function <m>f</m> and how we represent them symbolically:
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right) \amp =\frac{\partial^{2} f}{\partial x^{2}}=f_{x x}</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right) \amp =\frac{\partial^{2} f}{\partial y^{2}}=f_{y y}</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right) \amp =\frac{\partial^{2} f}{\partial y \partial x}=f_{x y}</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right) \amp =\frac{\partial^{2} f}{\partial x \partial y}=f_{y x}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-02-ex-8-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all four second-order partial derivatives of
        <m>z= \cos(x) \ln y</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first compute the two first-order partial derivatives, treating
        <m>y</m> and <m>x</m> as constants respectively:
        <md>
          z_x = -\sin(x) \ln y, \quad z_y = \frac{\cos(x)}{y}
        </md>
        Differentiating each of these once more, with respect to each
        variable:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>z_{xx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( -\sin(x) \ln y \right) = -\cos(x) \ln y</mrow>
          <mrow>z_{yy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left( \frac{\cos(x)}{y} \right) = -\frac{\cos(x)}{y^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>z_{xy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left( -\sin(x) \ln y \right) = -\frac{\sin(x)}{y}</mrow>
          <mrow>z_{yx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( \frac{\cos(x)}{y} \right) = -\frac{\sin(x)}{y}</mrow>
        </md>
        Note that the two mixed partial derivatives are equal,
        <m>z_{xy} = z_{yx}</m>, as expected since both are continuous
        functions (wherever <m>y \gt 0</m>, which is the domain of <m>z</m>).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Note that in <xref ref="example-ch03-02-ex-8-8"/>,
      <m>z_{x y}=z_{y x}</m>. This isn't always the case, but it is the case
      if <m>z_{x y}</m> and <m>z_{y x}</m> are continuous functions.
    </p>
  </note>

</section>
